My own strategy is to vary it.  If I'm playing a fighter role,
and have good weapons, I'll often go down to minetown first,
taking it fairly slowly, however, and always ready to retreat if
it starts looking too tough.  (As soon as I'm burdened with the
junk, I'll usually return upstairs before letting my pet check
it, then trying it on.  I don't particularly like taking my
armor off in the mines.)  If I'm playing less of a fighter role,
I'll generally go down the main branch until my pet is big, then
come back up and do the mines with it.  Unless, of course, my
pet dies early---I've had it die on the first move or so, by
falling into a pit.  Then my first job is to find a new pet.
(I don't make much use of pets globally, but I find them
essential in the starting phase to check for cursedness, and
unless I'm a Valk or Samurai, they also do most of the fighting
until I've got decent armor and weapons.)

I think maybe the thing I like most about nethack is that there
isn't one best strategy, and that what works perfectly one time
can get you into trouble the next.  (At least at the level I
play, I learn some new way to get into trouble in just about
every game.)

